      "b3d_place_h": "Where do your hands go?",
      "b3d_place_p": "Tap a spot on the chest in the 3D view, or pick from the list. Both do the same thing.",
      "b3d_rate": "And the rate",
      "b3d_rate_body": "100 to 120 compressions a minute for every age, which is faster than most people expect. The CPR + AED module has a rhythm trainer for exactly this. Keep interruptions as short as you can, and if an AED arrives, turn it on and do what it says.",
      "b3d_rec_done": "Positioned — now keep watching",
      "b3d_rec_done_body": "The recovery position buys a protected airway; it does not end the emergency. Stay with them, keep checking that breathing is still normal, and if it stops or turns to gasping, roll them onto their back and start compressions straight away.",
      "b3d_rec_h": "Recovery position, in order",
      "b3d_rec_p": "Only for someone unresponsive who IS breathing normally. Work through it and watch the body turn.",
      "b3d_rec_reset": "↺ Start again",
      "b3d_scope": "lay rescuer, compression-focused, covering adult, child and infant. This is orientation and practice, not certification — it cannot tell you whether your depth is real, which is exactly what an instructor with a manikin can. Take a hands-on course. In a real emergency, call 911 first or send someone to.",
