      "each_dot_is_a_city_where_a_printed_edi": "Each dot is a city where a printed edition of the Theses or an early Luther pamphlet appeared. Click play to watch the spread by month. By comparison, Wycliffe's manuscript-era writings (1380s) took decades to reach a fraction of these cities.",
      "each_dot_is_one_event_in_print_history": "Each dot is one event in print history, colored by region. The Gutenberg Bible (1455) is one mark among many.",
      "each_marker_is_a_moment_when_print_ena": "Each marker is a moment when print enabled something that would otherwise have stayed local, slow, or impossible. Click to learn what changed.",
      "each_master_offers_different_training_": "Each master offers different training, different working conditions, different opportunities. Read carefully. Once you choose you cannot change.",
      "each_milestone_collapses_one_bottlenec": "Each milestone collapses one bottleneck. The screw press scaled up the impression rate; the iron press eliminated frame failure; steam power broke human-arm throughput; Linotype broke composing-by-hand; desktop publishing put the shop on a desk; AI is trying to break composing entirely. The arc is six centuries of unbottlenecking the same job.",
      "each_round_the_pamphlet_finds_new_audi": "Each round, the pamphlet finds new audiences — read aloud somewhere else, lent to a neighbor, reprinted in another town.",
      "earl_stanhope_replaced_the_wooden_fram": "Earl Stanhope replaced the wooden frame with cast iron. The press could handle larger sheets and last for decades without splitting. Same throughput, much higher reliability and page size.",
